.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt combinesummaries 1
.Os combinesummaries
.\" ###### Name #############################################################
.Sh NAME
.Nm combinesummaries
.Nd Scalar Summary Combination Tool
.\" ###### Synopsis #########################################################
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm combinesummaries
.Ar output_file
.br
.Op Fl c Ar level | Fl \-compress Ar level
.br
.Op Fl s Ar separator | Fl \-separator Ar separator
.br
.Op Fl l | Fl \-line\-numbers | Fl n | Fl \-no\-line\-numbers
.br
.Op Fl q | Fl \-quiet
.br
.Op Ar variable_names
.Nm combinesummaries
.Op Fl h | Fl \-help
.Nm combinesummaries
.Op Fl v | Fl \-version
.\" ###### Description ######################################################
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm combinesummaries
is a combination tool for CSV/GNU R data files: it creates a single data table from multiple input files.
.Nm
supports on\-the\-fly compression with BZip2, GZip, XZ, and Zstandard. The format of the output file and input files is detected automatically from file extensions. After startup, the program accepts the following commands from standard input
.Pq Em not as command\-line arguments! :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm \-\-varnames= Ns Ar variables
A space\-separated list of output variable names to be added to the output data tables.
.It Cm \-\-values= Ns Ar values
Sets the values of the output variables for the next input file to the provided space\-separated values. One value for each variable has to be provided.
.It Cm \-\-input= Ns Ar filename
Process the given input file.
.It Cm \-\-simulationsdirectory= Ns Ar directory
Sets the directory where to find the input files.
.El
.Pp
.\" ###### Arguments ########################################################
.Sh ARGUMENTS
The following arguments and options may be provided:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ar output_file
The name of the output file to be created. The compression format is detected automatically from the file extension (e.g. .bz2, .gz, .xz, or .zst).
.It Ar variable_names
A space\-separated list of output variable names to be added to the output data tables.
.It Fl c Ar level | Fl \-compress Ar level
Sets the compression level. The valid range depends on the output format (e.g. 1\-9 for BZip2, 1\-22 for Zstandard, 0 for Plain). If not specified, the format's default compression level is used.
.It Fl s Ar separator | Fl \-separator Ar separator
Sets the separator for the tables. Default: tabulator character (i.e., $'\et').
.It Fl l | Fl \-line\-numbers
Generate line numbers for output rows (GNU R: row names). In this case, the input files must contain line numbers (GNU R: row names) as well! The original line numbers are kept in column SubLineNo. Default: off.
.It Fl n | Fl \-no\-line\-numbers
Do not add line numbers into the output table files, as usual for CSV (default).
.It Fl q | Fl \-quiet
Do not print verbose status information.
.It Fl h | Fl \-help
Prints command\-line help.
.It Fl v | Fl \-version
Prints program version.
.El
.\" ###### Exit status ######################################################
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
tool exits with 0 on success, and >0 in case of an error.
.\" ###### Examples #########################################################
.Sh EXAMPLES
Consider two data tables:
.Pa v1.data.bz2
(created with ParameterY=Alpha) and
.Pa v2.data.bz2
(created with ParameterY=Beta).
The output data table should be written to
.Pa output.data.zst
using Zstandard compression with compression level 5:
.Bd -literal
( echo \-e "\-\-values=\e\[dq]Alpha\e\[dq]\et\e\[dq]Test 1\e\[dq]" && \\
  echo "\-\-input=v1.data.bz2"               && \\
  echo \-e "\-\-values=\e\[dq]Beta\e\[dq]\et\e\[dq]Test 2\e\[dq]"  && \\
  echo "\-\-input=v2.data.bz2" ) | combinesummaries \-\-compress 5 output.data.zst $'ParameterX\etParameterY'
.Ed
.Pp
Print command\-line help:
.Bd -literal
combinesummaries \-\-help
.Ed
.Pp
Print program version:
.Bd -literal
combinesummaries \-\-version
.Ed
